<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pandavas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pandavas are the five brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a>, Bhima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a>, Nakula and Sahadeva, heroes of the Indian epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>. Sons of King Pandu's wives Kunti and Madri, they lose their kingdom to their cousins the Kauravas, spend years in exile, including a final year in disguise at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virata">Virata</a> of Matsya, and win it back in the war at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandavas.jpg" alt="Pandavas" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raja Ravi Varma · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Draupadi_and_Pandavas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name Pandava means descendants of Pandu. According to the Adi Parva of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, Pandu was crowned king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuru-kingdom">Kuru kingdom</a> although he was younger than his blind brother Dhritarashtra. Cursed by a sage to die if he touched a wife in desire, he withdrew to the forest with his wives Kunti and Madri. Kunti had received a boon from the sage Durvasa allowing her to have a son by any god, and at Pandu's request she used it and shared her mantra with Madri.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a> was fathered by the god Dharmadeva, Bhima by Vayu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and the twins Nakula and Sahadeva by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashvins">Ashvins</a>. After Pandu's death Kunti brought the boys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastinapura">Hastinapura</a>, where they were raised with their cousins the Kauravas, the hundred sons of Dhritarashtra.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Duryodhana, the eldest Kaurava, resented his cousins. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a> was named crown prince, Duryodhana and his uncle Shakuni had a palace of flammable lac built at Varnavarta, but Vidura warned the Pandavas and they escaped by a tunnel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> then won the hand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a>, princess of Panchala, in an archery contest, and all five brothers married her. Dhritarashtra partitioned the kingdom and gave them barren Khandavaprastha, which they built into the city of Indraprastha.</p><p>Shakuni then drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a> into a game of dice, in which he lost his kingdom, his brothers, himself and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a>. In a last game the loser was to spend twelve years in exile and a thirteenth in disguise, and the Pandavas lost. They spent that year as servants at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virata">Virata</a> of Matsya, and when they returned the Kauravas refused to give back the kingdom.</p><p>The Kurukshetra War lasted eighteen days. All hundred Kauravas and their army were killed, and the Pandavas, aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, won at great cost. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a> was crowned, and the brothers ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastinapura">Hastinapura</a> for thirty-six years before setting out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/draupadi">Draupadi</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kailash">Mount Kailash</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Harivamsa Purana, of the 8th century CE, tells the Jain version of their story. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> villagers perform the Pandav Lila, a ritual re-enactment of episodes from the epic in song, dance and recitation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pandava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the throne of Hastinapura, ruled by the Kuru clan, between two branches of the family: the Kauravas, the hundred sons of the blind king Dhritarashtra led by Duryodhana, and the <b>Pandavas</b>, the five sons of Pandu, Yudhishthira, Bhima, Arjuna, Nakula and Sahadeva, who all marry the princess Draupadi.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>They took their name from Arjuna, the <b>Pandava</b> hero of the Mahabharata, and appear to have emerged as an independent gana in the Bharatpur and Alwar region after the fall of the Maurya Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/arjunayanas">Arjunayanas</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Mahabharata King Virata of Matsya shelters the <b>Pandava</b> brothers in disguise during their thirteenth year of exile, and tradition derived the capital's name from him.</blockquote><a href="/state/matsya">Matsya</a></li></ul></section>
